TY  - JOUR
TI  - The subtlety of optimal paternalism in a population with bounded rationality
AU  - Manski, Charles F.
AU  - Sheshinski, Eytan
PY  - 2026
T2  - Social Choice and Welfare
AB  - We study optimal paternalism when a utilitarian planner designs a discrete choice set for a heterogeneous population with bounded rationality. We first consider the planning problem in abstraction. We show that the policy that most effectively constrains or influences choices depends multiplicatively on the preferences of the population and the choice probabilities conditional on preferences that characterize behavior. The dependence of social welfare on the joint distribution of preferences and deviations from rationality makes analysis of optimal paternalism subtle. We then study two settings in which the planner may mandate an action or decentralize decision making. One setting supposes that individuals measure utility with additive error and maximize mismeasured rather than actual utility. Then optimal planning requires knowledge of the distribution of measurement errors. The other setting studies treatment choice when the planner can mandate a treatment conditional on publicly observed personal covariates or can enable individuals to choose treatments using private information. We consider situations where bounded rationality stems from deviations between subjective and objective probabilities of uncertain outcomes. We use clinical decisions in medicine to illustrate. In toto, the paper shows that determination of optimal policy requires the planner to possess extensive knowledge that is rarely available. We caution that research in behavioral public economics should avoid incredible certitude when claiming to have determined optimal paternalistic policies.
